  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nAs of 06Z this morning, RAP surface analysis shows a high pressure \ncenter occupying the western portion of Quebec at 1030 mb, with \ntrailing 1027-28 mb over the UP. From GOES Nighttime Microphysics, \nthe more substantial cloud cover has moved south throughout the \nnight, leaving only high, thin clouds atop some parts of the CWA. \nWith over five hours yet to before sunrise, surface temperature \nobservations are starting to dip into the low 30s, with most \nremaining in the high 30s or low 40s (excepting those within \nlakeshore vicinity, which sit closer to 50F). Frost and Freeze \nheadlines are currently in effect, and temperatures are currently on \ntrack to achieve widespread frost in interior locations. Over land, \nwinds have gone largely calm for the night except for some coastal \nlocations which show winds mostly less than 10 mph in a land breeze \npattern.\n\nThe robust Quebec high slowly propagates east through the day today \nwhile persistent ridging remains over the Upper Great Lakes. Light, \neasterly flow continues as the deepening Canadian high pressure \ndeflects a compact low spinning toward the Southern Great Lakes. \nWith highs in the low to mid 60s today, a slightly warmer low-level \ntemperature profile in the latter half of the day will cause frost \nformation to struggle a bit more despite efficient (clear sky) \ncooling. The NBM shows a similar but slightly smaller footprint for \nfrost tomorrow morning, with the areas of widespread frost \nrestricted relative to the ongoing frost event. That said, areas of \nfrost are likely in some portions of interior west (50-90%) and \npatchy frost is possible elsewhere (20-60%). Later this week, as \nsurface flow turns southerly, overnight lows on Weds night/Thurs \nmorning are unlikely to catalyze frost formation.\n\nWith dry air in place aloft, widespread relative humidities of 30-\n35% are likely Tuesday late afternoon. Gaylord's 00Z sounding shows \ndeep-layer dry air between 850 and 500 mb; modeled soundings \ncontinue to predict dewpoint depressions in excess of 20C for this \nafternoon. However, given light winds and highs in the 60s, \nconditions do not meet criteria for even borderline or limited fire \nweather concerns. Easterly flow persists tomorrow before beginning \nto veer southerly, leading to increasing low-level moisture \nWednesday and Thursday, with minimum RHs in the 35-40% range on \nWednesday and over 50% on Thursday.\n\nFurther out, though some low chances of precip brush the western \nedge of the CWA in the Thurs-Fri timeframe, the remainder of the \nweek is setup to remain dry, with good large-scale agreement amongst \nensembles into Saturday. The next chance for potentially meaningful \nprecipitation at this time is late on Saturday into Sunday, in \nconjunction with a mid-level trough entering the PNW later this week.",
